//! core/net/websocket — dialling somebody else's WebSocket.
//!
//! `core/net/server` is the accepting half: a program that holds a port, takes
//! an upgrade request and runs three hooks over the socket it made. This is the
//! other half — a program that *dials* a socket somebody else is holding. The
//! hooks are the same three, the state threads the same way, and `Socket`,
//! `Message` and `CloseReason` are that module's own types re-exported here, so
//! a program that has written one end already knows the other.
//!
//! ```buri
//! from "core/effect" import { Sockets, Stdout, WebSocketClient };
//! from "core/host" import * as host;
//! from "core/io" import * as io;
//! from "core/net/websocket" import * as websocket;
//!
//! export fn main(): Result<(), Str> {
//!     let ctx = context {
//!         Stdout: host.stdout,
//!         Sockets: host.sockets,
//!         WebSocketClient: host.websocketClient,
//!     };
//!     websocket
//!         .connect(ctx, websocket.Client {
//!             url: "ws://127.0.0.1:3000/socket",
//!             onOpen: fn(c, socket, _response) => {
//!                 let _sent = socket.send(c, .Text("hello"));
//!                 0
//!             },
//!             onMessage: fn(c, socket, count, _message) => {
//!                 let _closed = socket.close(c, .Normal);
//!                 count + 1
//!             },
//!             onClose: fn(c, _socket, count, _reason) => {
//!                 io.println(c, "got ${count} frames").ignore()
//!             },
//!         })
//!         .map(fn(_reason) => ())
//!         .mapErr(websocket.errorText)
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! ## `connect` returns when the socket closes
//!
//! There is one call and it is the socket's whole life: dial, run `onOpen`, read
//! a message and run `onMessage` for each one, and run `onClose` when the far
//! side goes or this end closes. The answer is the `CloseReason` it ended with.
//!
//! `.Err` means the socket was never opened — a URL this platform cannot dial, a
//! machine that refused the connection, a server that did not answer `101`. Once
//! a socket exists every ending is an `.Ok` carrying a reason, because a socket
//! closing is not a failure.
//!
//! **Reconnecting is a loop around it.** There is no reconnect knob, no backoff
//! setting and no retry count, because `connect` is an ordinary call that
//! returns:
//!
//! ```buri
//! from "core/effect" import { Clock, Sockets, WebSocketClient };
//! from "core/net/server" import { CloseReason };
//! from "core/net/websocket" import * as websocket;
//! from "core/net/websocket" import { Client };
//! from "core/time" import * as time;
//!
//! export fn following<C: Clock + Sockets + WebSocketClient>(
//!     ctx: C,
//!     client: Client<C, Int>,
//!     tries: Int,
//! ): CloseReason {
//!     match (websocket.connect(ctx, client)) {
//!         .Ok(reason) => reason,
//!         .Err(_unreachable) => {
//!             if (tries <= 0) {
//!                 .Abnormal
//!             } else {
//!                 let _slept = time.sleepMs(ctx, 1000);
//!                 following(ctx, client, tries - 1)
//!             }
//!         },
//!     }
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! ## What the hooks see
//!
//! `onOpen` is handed the `Response` that opened the socket, where
//! `core/net/server`'s is handed the `Request` that asked for one. It is the
//! same value from the other side of the wire, and it is where a negotiated
//! subprotocol arrives.
//!
//! Everything else reads the same as a server's hooks. `onOpen` answers the
//! socket's first state, every later hook is handed the current one, and
//! `onMessage` answers the next. `onClose` runs once, with the last state, and
//! is the last thing the socket does — the socket is already closed by then, so
//! anything sent from there is dropped. `onMessage` sees `.Text` and `.Binary`
//! and nothing else, because a ping and a pong are the platform keeping the
//! connection alive.
//!
//! **Pushing needs `Sockets` and nothing else.** A `Socket` here is the same
//! inert integer a server's is, so it can go to an actor and be pushed on from
//! there. That is why `connect`'s bound is `WebSocketClient + Sockets`: the
//! first dials and the second writes.
//!
//! ## Headers and subprotocols are the URL's business
//!
//! `Client` has a `url` and no header list. A browser's `WebSocket` cannot send
//! request headers at all — that is the platform's rule and not this module's —
//! so a header list here would be a field that worked on the two native
//! platforms and nowhere else. A program that has to authenticate puts the token
//! in the query string, which is what every browser client does, and reads what
//! it was given back off the response in `onOpen`.
//!
//! ## On a page, and in a worker
//!
//! `connect` follows `ui.mount`: it suspends without holding the event loop, so
//! a page that mounted an interface and then dialled a socket goes on rendering
//! while the socket is idle. A pushed frame wakes it exactly as a click does. A
//! worker dials the same way while it is answering a request.
//!
//! What differs there is who writes the handshake. On `LINUX` and `MACOS` this
//! runtime writes it and checks every clause of the answer. Everywhere else the
//! platform's own `WebSocket` does, so how strictly a bad `101` is refused is
//! that engine's decision, and `Connected.headers` carries what the engine
//! exposes — the negotiated subprotocol and extensions — rather than the head
//! the server sent.

from "core/effect" import {
    Connected, Received, Response, ServeError, Sockets, WebSocketClient,
};
from "core/net/server" import { CloseReason, Message, Socket };

from "core/effect" export { Connected, Response, ServeError };

from "core/net/server" export { CloseReason, errorText, Message, Socket };

/// A client, whole: where to dial, and the three hooks over the socket it gets.
///
/// `C` is the caller's own context and every hook is handed it, so a hook may do
/// anything the context it was written beside can do. `S` is **the state this
/// socket carries**: `onOpen` answers the first one, every later hook is handed
/// the current one, and `onMessage` answers the next. So per-socket state is a
/// value on a stack rather than a table keyed by socket — `core/net/server`'s
/// `WebSocket` says the same thing about the same three hooks, and one program
/// can use both.
export struct Client<C, S> {
    /// Where to dial. `ws://…` or `wss://…`; anything else is
    /// `.Err(.Unsupported)` from `connect`.
    ///
    /// **The whole of the request.** A subprotocol and a token go in the query
    /// string, because a page cannot send a header on a WebSocket handshake —
    /// see the module header.
    export url: Str,
    /// The socket is open. Answers its first state.
    ///
    /// The `Response` is the `101` the server answered — its status and its
    /// header fields — and it is where a negotiated subprotocol arrives. It is
    /// the mirror of `core/net/server`'s `onOpen`, which is handed the `Request`
    /// that asked.
    export onOpen: fn(C, Socket, Response) => S,
    /// A message arrived. Answers the state the next one will see.
    export onMessage: fn(C, Socket, S, Message) => S,
    /// The socket is gone. Runs once, with the last state.
    ///
    /// **The socket is already closed when this runs**, so anything sent from
    /// here is dropped. Use it to undo what `onOpen` did.
    export onClose: fn(C, Socket, S, CloseReason) => (),
}

/// Dial, run the hooks, and answer the reason the socket ended.
///
/// One call and one socket. It returns when the socket closes, so a program that
/// wants a second one calls it again — the module header writes that loop out.
///
/// `.Err` is a socket that never opened. Everything after the handshake is
/// `.Ok`, because a socket closing is the ordinary end of one and not a failure:
/// `.Ok(.Normal)` is a clean goodbye and `.Ok(.Abnormal)` is a connection that
/// dropped, and a program that cares reads the reason.
///
/// **`Sockets` is in the bound because the hooks push.** `connect` itself never
/// calls a `Sockets` method — `socket.send` and `socket.close` are the hooks' —
/// but the hooks are handed this context, so the authority has to be in it. That
/// is the same reason `core/net/server`'s `serve` carries `Tasks`.
export fn connect<C: WebSocketClient + Sockets, S>(
    ctx: C,
    client: Client<C, S>,
): Result<CloseReason, ServeError> {
    match (ctx.connectSocket(client.url)) {
        .Err(e) => .Err(e),
        .Ok(connected) => .Ok(opened(ctx, client, connected)),
    }
}

/// A socket, from the hook that opens it to the hook that closes it.
///
/// The `Response` is built here, out of the three fields `Connected` carries
/// flat. That is the division `core/effect` states where `Connected` is
/// declared: the runtime writes one flat shape and the program reads the value
/// it wanted.
fn opened<C: WebSocketClient, S>(
    ctx: C,
    client: Client<C, S>,
    connected: Connected,
): CloseReason {
    let socket = Socket(connected.socket);
    let open = client.onOpen;
    let state = open(ctx, socket, Response {
        status: connected.status,
        headers: connected.headers,
        body: connected.body,
    });
    pumping(ctx, client, socket, state)
}

/// One message, then the next, then the close.
///
/// A self tail call, so a socket that carries a million messages costs one frame
/// — `core/net/server`'s `pumping` is the same loop from the other end, and
/// `middle/tail_calls.rs` is what makes it one (design/non-goals.md).
///
/// **`.Err` is a close too.** A socket that is already gone when this asks
/// reaches `onClose` as `.Abnormal` rather than getting dropped. The hook is
/// where a program undoes what `onOpen` did, and a socket that skipped it would
/// leak whatever that was.
fn pumping<C: WebSocketClient, S>(
    ctx: C,
    client: Client<C, S>,
    socket: Socket,
    state: S,
): CloseReason {
    match (ctx.connectReceive(socket.0)) {
        .Err(_gone) => closed(ctx, client, socket, state, .Abnormal),
        .Ok(received) => stepped(ctx, client, socket, state, received),
    }
}

/// One thing that arrived, as the hook it runs.
fn stepped<C: WebSocketClient, S>(
    ctx: C,
    client: Client<C, S>,
    socket: Socket,
    state: S,
    received: Received,
): CloseReason {
    match (received.frame) {
        .Closed => closed(ctx, client, socket, state, reasonOf(received.code)),
        .Text => {
            let step = client.onMessage;
            pumping(ctx, client, socket, step(ctx, socket, state, .Text(received.text)))
        },
        .Binary => {
            let step = client.onMessage;
            pumping(
                ctx,
                client,
                socket,
                step(ctx, socket, state, .Binary(received.data)),
            )
        },
    }
}

/// The close hook, run once, with the state the last message left, and the
/// reason `connect` answers.
fn closed<C, S>(
    ctx: C,
    client: Client<C, S>,
    socket: Socket,
    state: S,
    reason: CloseReason,
): CloseReason {
    let close = client.onClose;
    let _closed = close(ctx, socket, state, reason);
    reason
}

/// The number a socket closed with, as the reason a program reads.
///
/// **The same mapping `core/net/server` applies to a server's sockets**, and the
/// second copy of it in this language. It is private in both modules on purpose:
/// a close code is what crosses to a platform, and "wire codes never appear in
/// Buri code" is a rule about the code people write. Exporting either copy would
/// hand a program the numbers back.
///
/// A negative code is the platform's own and never the far side's — wire codes
/// are unsigned — so nothing a server can send reaches the first arm. Every
/// number this enum does not name is `.Abnormal`, including 1005 and 1006.
fn reasonOf(code: Int): CloseReason {
    if (code < 0) {
        .Overflow
    } else {
        match (code) {
            1000 => .Normal,
            1001 => .GoingAway,
            1002 => .ProtocolError,
            1003 => .Unsupported,
            1008 => .Policy,
            1009 => .TooLarge,
            1011 => .InternalError,
            _ => .Abnormal,
        }
    }
}
